<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oyo Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oyo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oyo Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> of present-day western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, founded according to tradition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a>. Its cavalry-based military enabled it to dominate a tributary network extending to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> and the coast, especially in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Weakened by internal conflict and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, it collapsed around 1835–1836.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oyo-empire.jpg" alt="Oyo Empire" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry B. Lovejoy; “Old Oyo Influences on the Transformation of Lucumí Identity in Colonial Cuba”, UCLA Electronic Theses and … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontiers_of_the_Kingdom_of_Oyo_and_its_neighbours_in_c._1790.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to oral tradition the origins of Oyo lie with Ọranmiyan, the last prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> kingdom of Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>. He agreed with his brother to launch a punitive raid on their northern neighbours for insulting their father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, the first Ooni of Ife, but the brothers quarrelled on the way and the army split up. Ọranmiyan's force was too small to attack, and he wandered along the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> until he reached Bussa. He became the first oba of Oyo and was succeeded by Ajaka, then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a>, later deified as the deity of thunder and lightning. The founding is placed at about 1300, but this is only estimated by backtracking through the chronology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> of Oyo spent an interregnum of 80 years as an exiled dynasty. Re-established in the early 17th century, Oyo became more centralised and more militant than before. By 1680 it spanned over 150,000 square kilometres, held together by mutual self-interest and by a combination of local autonomy and imperial authority. Islamic influence in the empire was slight, though some Muslim officials were kept at Oyo.</p><p>Many believe the decline began as early as 1754, with the palace intrigues of the prime minister Gaha. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-mesi">Oyo Mesi</a> he forced four successive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafins</a> to commit ritual suicide, two of them between June and October 1754 alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abiodun">Alaafin Abiodun</a> had Gaha executed in 1774, but the instability had further weakened Oyo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a>, whose title means owner of the palace, was head of the empire and supreme overlord. He kept tributaries safe, settled disputes between sub-rulers, and received their homage at annual ceremonies, of which the Bere festival was the most important. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-mesi">Oyo Mesi</a>, who spoke for the politicians, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogboni-society">Ogboni</a>, an earth cult speaking for the people, kept his power in check. The empire had four layers: metropolitan Oyo, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>, the Egbado Corridor, which secured trade with the coast, and the distant Ajaland, reaching far into Ewe territory in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>.</p><p>Its army relied on cavalry, as most of its territory lay in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a>. Horses were bought from the north and kept in metropolitan Oyo, which was partly free of the tsetse fly. Oyo also became the southern emporium of the trans-Saharan trade, exchanging salt, leather, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, cloth and slaves, and it often demanded tribute in slaves from its tributaries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oyo Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Old Yoruba cities such as Old Oyo (Oyo-ile), Ijaye and Owu disappeared in those years, and newer ones, Abeokuta, New Oyo (<b>Oyo</b> Atiba) and Ibadan, rose to replace them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ibadan">Ibadan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>These conquests provoked repeated invasions by the cavalry of the <b>Oyo Empire</b>, and around 1730 Agaja accepted tributary status under Oyo, a relationship that lasted almost a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Oduduwa's sons, daughters and grandchildren founded their own kingdoms and empires: Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ijesha and <b>Oyo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later operations extended the caliphate southward into Yoruba territory: Ilorin, where the <b>Oyo</b> general Afonja rebelled around 1817 with Fulani and Muslim support, passed under a Fulani emir in the 1820s and, as the Ilorin Emirate, became the caliphate's spearhead against the Oyo Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Oyo Empire</b>, which from the 17th century dominated much of the Yoruba country and the lands to the west, spread the worship of Shango, the orisha of thunder, remembered in tradition as an early king of Oyo.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ilorin's cavalry raided deep into Yorubaland and helped destroy the old <b>Oyo</b> capital in the 1830s, but Ibadan defeated it at Osogbo around 1840, halting the jihad's advance south.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
